10aba-e2a2-4977-99b7-38e8e61667b8"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[],"created_at":1787080961935970001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[{"link_uuid":"23754325-87fa-426b-97f1-b30a0ad91b04","relation":"depends_on","target_id":"60c1-80de-a22f-4d39"},{"link_uuid":"874c5875-62a0-43f9-a96e-a7b91ac40a30","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"5eef-df65-8229-4a5d"},{"link_uuid":"6a77e341-498c-4b3e-bf32-0017118f47c9","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"d6dc-da93-ad4f-46eb"},{"link_uuid":"ce134757-b2ef-4c7c-bcfb-ba5b7ef69355","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"e327-b494-ed24-4c19"}],"description":"## Context / Problem\nBoth review gates execute criterion work through runners whose return values cannot distinguish clean execution from failed, cancelled, skipped, budget-shed, or resumed work. Plan-review failures can become empty successful checkpoints; code review loses earlier work when a later criterion raises. An effective-policy snapshot cannot fix those execution facts.\n\n## Approach\nAdd a narrow shared discovery kernel as a NEW `discovery` submodule under `rebar.llm.review_kernel`, independent of that package's four-pass DECISION kernel (find/verify/decide/coach): it neither imports nor modifies `verify`/`decide`/`coach`/`verify_models`, and `__init__` gains additive exports only.\n\nDefine immutable `DiscoveryStagePlan`/`DiscoveryUnitPlan` inputs plus `DiscoveryStageResult` and typed per-unit outcomes (`success`, `resumed`, `skipped`, `shed`, `failed`, `cancelled`). A plan carries stable unit ids, dependencies, prompt/contract/model/mode identities, context/policy digests, blocking capability, budget estimate, and gate-supplied execution callbacks.\n\nThe executor owns dependency-aware scheduling, explicit positive-budget shedding, usage accumulation, cancellation observation, and success-only checkpoint envelope creation. Shedding order is deterministic — ascending dependency rank, then stable unit id; shedding a unit also sheds its unmet dependents as `skipped`, and the loop re-evaluates projected spend against the cap after each shed-plus-cascade, stopping once the kept set fits (minimal, identical across runs). After RP-01 exhausts a local operation it preserves successful independent results and continues; a dependent of missing work becomes `skipped` with a dependency reason. Systemic config/provider-init failures and cancellation abort remaining work with exact completed-so-far accounting. Verdicts remain outside the executor.\n\nDefine a new versioned, digest-complete checkpoint/trace envelope whose identity covers material, code/ref, effective-policy snapshot, prompt and contract, model/mode, topology, input context, dependencies, and a namespace version — the discovery contract/schema version, a constant in `discovery` bumped when the envelope schema changes, so identity is deterministic and a mismatched-or-absent namespace version is legacy and missing. Only `success` and content-identical `resumed` units commit as reusable successes; failed, cancelled, skipped, and shed units remain missing.\n\nThe typed trace is safe stored diagnostic data — normalized ids, outcomes, reason codes, usage, lineage only; it excludes raw prompts, ticket/context bodies, provider responses, and secrets. No per-unit trace is added to review/review-status contracts.\n\n## Boundaries and Rationale\nKeep policy selection, gate context construction, YAML/BPMN topology, provider-turn retry/output, checkpoint persistence, and final verdicts in their current owners.\n\nPrior-art reconciliation — the kernel defines unified typed successors that RP-06's migration stories move the gates onto; current implementations stay unchanged until then:\n- Budget: `sizing.shed_to_budget` treats `cap_override=None` as the centrality-scaled COMPUTED cap (not uncapped), `0.0` as a valid \"shed everything\" cap, and never sheds container (G3/G4) criteria. The kernel deliberately defines the simpler AC4 contract; S3 owns mapping `REBAR_PLAN_REVIEW_BUDGET`/`cap_override` and container-never-shed on explicitly, so no cost posture changes silently here.\n- Checkpoint/resume: `sizing.load_checkpoint`/`save_checkpoint`/`_checkpoint_key` and `pass1.run_pass1` are chunk-keyed and can round-trip an empty-but-\"successful\" checkpoint; the versioned digest-complete envelope supersedes that keying (existing functions remaining until S3), and `run_pass1`'s independent finding-extension + usage aggregation is restated as typed outcomes so a later-raising unit cannot erase earlier successes.\n- Cancellation: the cut point is reused verbatim from `generation.review_cancelled()` (observed BEFORE dispatch; an already-dispatched non-interruptible call is sunk cost and commits as its true `success`/`failed` outcome with real usage; a not-yet-dispatched unit is `cancelled`), so no new mechanism and AC3's accounting is total.\n- Scheduling vs `workflow/executor.py`: the discovery kernel is a thin linear SYNCHRONOUS pass ordering review-criterion units by `graphlib.static_order` — the SAME primitive and the same no-asyncio/threads/retry discipline ADR 0065's Burr tripwire protects — not a second scheduler. It does not reuse that executor because the domains differ (workflow steps carry `${{ steps.x.outputs }}` templating, registry dispatch, WORKFLOW_RUN/STEP RunState/RunRecorder; discovery units carry typed outcomes, shedding, the envelope). Shared primitive reused, interpreter not forked; no ADR 0065 trigger fires.\n\nThe per-unit dependency graph and `skipped`-with-dependency-reason are the shared-substrate contract, NOT a current gate edge: today plan-review Pass-1 chunks/agent criteria fan out INDEPENDENTLY (a failed chunk merely counted in `chunk_errors`) and code-review runs each criterion independently — so for both current gates the graph is EMPTY and scheduling is independent fan-out. The typed edge exists so a migration story composing a unit that consumes another's result expresses a skip-on-missing dependency as a first-class `skipped` outcome, not a silently-emptied success. Introducing it now, empty-graph path tested, keeps the envelope's dependency digest stable across migrations.\n\n## Testing\nIn-process contract tests with the real plan/result/envelope codecs and an injected stateful fake only at the model-call boundary, asserting exact call order, outcomes, usage, checkpoint eligibility, collateral state, and a clean retry after injected local failure. Negative controls prove a systemic failure aborts and a failed/shed/cancelled result never round trips as success. Covered: the empty-graph fan-out path; a cancellation where an in-flight call completing after the signal commits as its true outcome while a not-yet-dispatched sibling is `cancelled`; a budget case with an identical shed set across runs, zero rejected, omission uncapped.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] `DiscoveryStagePlan` and `DiscoveryStageResult` represent stable unit identity, dependencies, blocking capability, usage, and all six outcome kinds without gate-specific verdict fields.\n- [x] The discovery contracts live in a new `rebar.llm.review_kernel.discovery` submodule with additive `__init__` exports and no change to the existing `verify`/`decide`/`coach`/`verify_models` symbols.\n- [x] Given one exhausted local failure, the real executor retains earlier successes, executes later independent units, and marks dependent units skipped with the missing dependency id.\n- [x] Given a systemic initialization/configuration failure or cancellation, the executor stops scheduling new work and returns exact completed, failed, and cancelled accounting without fabricating usage or findings; a unit whose non-interruptible call was already dispatched when cancellation is observed commits as its true `success`/`failed` outcome with real usage; a not-yet-dispatched unit is `cancelled`.\n- [x] A positive explicit budget deterministically sheds work that does not fit — in ascending (dependency rank, unit id) order so the shed set is identical across runs; omission is uncapped and zero is rejected rather than interpreted as disablement.\n- [x] The versioned envelope round-trips only digest-complete, content-identical success/resume records; injected failed, cancelled, skipped, shed, corrupt, and legacy envelopes stay missing.\n- [x] Stored trace serialization contains normalized outcome metadata and lineage but no raw prompt, ticket/context body, provider payload, or secret, and public review result schemas remain unchanged.\n- [x] Fault-injection tests prove restoration plus a clean rerun succeeds and unrelated success state is unchanged.","env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","file_impact":[{"path":"src/rebar/llm/review_kernel/discovery.py","reason":"Typed discovery plans, outcomes, executor, usage, and success-only envelope codec."},{"path":"src/rebar/llm/review_kernel/__init__.py","reason":"Expose the shared discovery contract without gate policy."},{"path":"tests/unit/test_review_discovery_executor.py","reason":"Dependency scheduling, partial failure, budget, cancellation, and usage contract tests."},{"path":"tests/unit/test_review_discovery_trace.py","reason":"Digest-complete envelope, secret-exclusion, corruption, legacy, and clean-rerun tests."}],"file_impact_scope":"paths","keyring":[],"last_status_env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","managed_refs":[["depends_on","60c1-80de-a22f-4d39"],["parent","58bd-d656-c52f-4cf4"],["relates_to","5eef-df65-8229-4a5d"],["relates_to","d6dc-da93-ad4f-46eb"],["relates_to","e327-b494-ed24-4c19"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":"58bd-d656-c5